{"answerArea":{"calculator":false,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"First, we note that $k=3$ makes the denominator zero and so cannot be a solution.\n\nNow, we multiply both sides of the equation by $3k-9$ to get rid of the quotient. ","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"$\\begin{align}\n\\dfrac{4k - 6}{3k - 9} \u0026= \\dfrac{1}{3}\n\\\\\\\\\n(3k-9)\\cdot \\dfrac{4k - 6}{3k - 9} \u0026= (3k-9)\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{3}\n\\\\\\\\\n4k-6\u0026=k-3\n\\\\\\\\\n3k\u0026=3\n\\\\\\\\\nk\u0026=1\n\\end{align}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"We get that $k=1$ is a potential solution. It doesn't make the denominator zero, so it is indeed a solution.\n\n**In conclusion, $k = 1$.**","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}}],"itemDataVersion":{"major":0,"minor":1},"question":{"content":"**Solve for $k$.**\n\n$\\dfrac{4k - 6}{3k - 9} = \\dfrac{1}{3}$\n\n$k =$ [[☃ numeric-input 1]]","images":{},"widgets":{"numeric-input 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"answers":[{"maxError":0,"message":"","simplify":"required","status":"correct","strict":false,"value":1}],"coefficient":false,"labelText":"","multipleNumberInput":false,"size":"normal","static":false},"static":false,"type":"numeric-input","version":{"major":0,"minor":0}}}}}
